WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.464
You already know how small analytics works.

00:00:05.464 --> 00:00:08.929
Pandas loads your whole dataset into your machine's RAM and works on it there.

00:00:08.929 --> 00:00:12.393
Read a file from disk, build a DataFrame, group, sort, and join, all in memory, and hand back a result fast.

00:00:12.393 --> 00:00:15.858
One process, one machine.

00:00:15.858 --> 00:00:19.322
But there is a wall.

00:00:19.322 --> 00:00:22.786
When the data is larger than RAM, the program does not slow down.

00:00:22.786 --> 00:00:26.251
It crashes.

00:00:26.251 --> 00:00:29.715
A laptop holds maybe sixteen gigabytes.

00:00:29.715 --> 00:00:33.180
Spotify holds billions of listens.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:36.644
Bridging those two numbers is the rest of this course.

00:00:36.644 --> 00:00:40.110
Make it concrete.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.575
One schema runs the whole course.

00:00:43.575 --> 00:00:47.041
Songs, about a hundred million of them.

00:00:47.041 --> 00:00:50.507
Listens, billions of rows, one per play.

00:00:50.507 --> 00:00:53.972
And Users.

00:00:53.972 --> 00:00:57.438
Take a common task: the top ten songs by play count.

00:00:57.438 --> 00:01:00.903
In Pandas you load the listens into a DataFrame, join, group by song, count, sort, and take ten.

00:01:00.903 --> 00:01:04.369
The same shape as a spreadsheet, or a query you once ran by hand.

00:01:04.369 --> 00:01:10.898
Here is the deeper idea, and it is the heart of this course.

00:01:10.898 --> 00:01:17.428
In plain Python you would write nested for-loops, build a dictionary of counts, and sort it yourself.

00:01:17.428 --> 00:01:23.957
You spell out how, step by step.

00:01:23.957 --> 00:01:30.487
Pandas, Polars, and SQL let you describe what you want instead, the top ten by play count, and the engine decides how to run it.

00:01:30.487 --> 00:01:37.016
That declarative model is what lets the very same query move from a laptop to a thousand-machine cluster, unchanged.

00:01:37.016 --> 00:01:42.868
Now watch the same query as the data grows.

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:48.719
Pandas loads every row into RAM with one core and copies at each step, so it dies the moment the data passes RAM.

00:01:48.719 --> 00:01:54.571
Polars keeps almost the same code but runs a lazy, streaming, all-cores plan that only materializes what it needs, which pushes the limit far out, but it is still one machine.

00:01:54.571 --> 00:02:00.422
A database flips the problem.

00:02:00.422 --> 00:02:06.274
It ships the query to the data, plans across many machines and disks, and returns just your rows.

00:02:06.274 --> 00:02:12.125
No single-machine limit at all.

00:02:12.125 --> 00:02:15.989
So where does this leave Pandas?

00:02:15.989 --> 00:02:19.853
Exactly where it belongs.

00:02:19.853 --> 00:02:23.716
Fast, familiar, and bounded by one machine.

00:02:23.716 --> 00:02:27.580
For data that fits, it is the right tool.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:31.444
The moment it does not fit, you reach for the thing this course is about: a database that spreads the work across many machines.

00:02:31.444 --> 00:02:37.280
Pandas and Polars are a database in the small.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:43.116
The same operations, group, sort, and join, but in RAM on one machine.

00:02:43.116 --> 00:02:48.952
The whole rest of the course is removing that one limit, making those operations work when the data is far bigger than any single machine's memory.
